From: ReadyNAS NG <readynasng@build>
Subject: [PATCH 28/28] ksmbd: zero-copy writes into the page cache

An SMB2 WRITE was received into a buffer of up to 4 MiB (kvmalloc, often
an order-9 kmalloc after direct compaction), copied from the socket into
it (copy 1), then copied into the page cache by kernel_write (copy 2).
Measured on the ReadyNAS (tick profile, 500 MiB written): copy 1 is 13-20 %
of the CPU, copy 2 17-26 %.

The receive loop now reads only the headers of a plain WRITE (the SMB2
header and the WRITE request up to its data: 112 bytes) and moves the data
from the socket into a pipe whose buffers reference the socket buffers
(tcp_read_sock + skb_splice_bits, nothing copied); the worker then splices
the pipe into the file with ->splice_write (iter_file_splice_write), the
filesystem's own copy into the page cache being the only one. The whole
PDU is consumed by the receive loop before the request is handed over, as
before: the worker checks the request (session, tree, credits, handle,
access, lock range) exactly as before, and the stream stays in sync
whatever it decides.

Only for WRITEs of 64 KiB or more, alone in their PDU (no compound),
unsigned (the signature covers the data in one buffer), not encrypted,
on TCP, with the data right after the headers. Otherwise, and when the pipe
cannot be had or fills up, the data goes into a buffer as before (copied
out of the pipe if it was already there); same for streams and files
without iter_file_splice_write. A 64 KiB write to a named pipe (IPC$) is
refused.

Run-time switch: /sys/module/ksmbd/parameters/zerocopy_write (default 1).
Measured on the NAS (500 MiB from a Wi-Fi PC, files compared byte for
byte, with 4 MiB pageblocks, see linux-padre-nas.fragment): unsigned
writes 12.8-13.5 MiB/s, 0.075-0.082 CPU s/MiB, against 10.4-11.3 MiB/s,
0.093-0.102 CPU s/MiB with the switch off (0.1.3: 9.9-11.3 MiB/s,
0.091-0.104 CPU s/MiB). Without the smaller pageblocks this path was
bimodal (up to 0.15 CPU s/MiB, the page allocator at 40 %).
